improves Cormack–Lehane grade ≥1 in~80% of cases</li><li><b>Bougie use:</b>98% vs 87% first-pass success vs. stylet (Driver et al., JAMA 2018 — RCT, n=757)</li><li><b>BURP</b>(Backward, Upward, Rightward Pressure) or bimanual laryngoscopy to optimise glottic view</li><li>Limit to <b>maximum 2 intubation attempts</b>before declaring failure and moving to Plan B</li><li>Ensure full neuromuscular blockade at each attempt (Train-of-Four=0)</li><li>Call for help before second attempt if first failed</li></ul><p style="margin-top:7px"><span class="tag">CL grade I–II=favourable</span><span class="tag w">CL grade III–IV=difficult</span></p></div></div><div class="branch"><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel by">SUCCESS</div><div class="arr g">↓</div><div class="box bplan" onclick="tog('d6a')"><div class="t">Confirm ETT — Waveform Capnography</div><div class="s">Gold-standard confirmation · CXR · Secure tube</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d6a"><h4>ETT Position Confirmation</h4><ul><li><b>Waveform capnography</b>— mandatory;
sensitivity and specificity 100% in perfusing rhythm (most reliable method)</li><li>Bilateral breath sounds auscultation (mid-axillary lines)</li><li>Symmetric chest rise</li><li>SpO₂ improvement trend</li><li>Chest X-ray: ETT tip should be 3–5 cm above carina</li><li>Document: tube size,
depth at lips,
Cormack–Lehane grade,
number of attempts,
devices used</li></ul></div></div><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel bn">FAILED ×2</div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="box bwarn" onclick="tog('d6b')"><div class="t">CALL FOR HELP · Declare "Cannot Intubate" </div><div class="s">Maintain oxygenation. Do not persist.</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d6b"><h4>After Failed Intubation (DAS 2015 — Transition to Plan B)</h4><ul><li>Stop attempting intubation — each additional attempt causes airway trauma and worsens conditions</li><li>Maintain oxygen delivery via face mask (two-person BVM technique if needed)</li><li>Call senior anaesthetist / ENT / airway team NOW — earlier is always better</li><li>Consider waking patient if elective procedure and haemodynamically stable</li><li>Declare failure aloud — cognitive aids and team debriefing improve outcomes</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="arr">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bwarn" onclick="tog('d7')"><div class="t"><span class="badge">PLAN B</span>Supraglottic Airway (SGA / LMA)</div><div class="s">2nd-generation SGA preferred · Max 2 attempts</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d7"><h4>Supraglottic Airway Devices</h4><ul><li><b>2nd-generation SGA</b>(i-gel, LMA ProSeal, LMA Supreme) preferred — incorporate gastric drainage channel,
significantly reducing aspiration risk</li><li>i-gel: no inflatable cuff;
insertion success rate~97%;
oropharyngeal seal pressure 24–26 cmH₂O</li><li>Can be used as conduit for bronchoscope-guided intubation (intubating LMA / ILMA Fastrach)</li><li>Size selection: female → 4;
male → 5 (weight-based guide on packaging)</li><li><b>Obstetric:</b>SGA is not a substitute for RSI in the full-stomach patient;
use only as rescue ventilation bridge</li><li><b>Pediatric:</b>i-gel validated ≥2 kg;
see Pediatric tab for weight-based sizing</li></ul></div></div><div class="branch"><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel by">VENTILATING</div><div class="arr g">↓</div><div class="box bplan" onclick="tog('d8a')"><div class="t">Maintain O₂ via SGA</div><div class="s">Awaken · Plan definitive airway when safe</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d8a"><h4>Options Once Oxygenating via SGA</h4><ul><li><b>Elective case:</b>Wake patient up → plan definitive airway (awake FOB) in controlled setting with full team</li><li><b>Emergency case:</b>Attempt intubation via SGA conduit with flexible bronchoscope,
or maintain as temporising measure until senior help arrives</li><li>Always document and communicate the difficult airway;
patient should carry a Difficult Airway Alert card</li><li><b>Obstetric:</b>if CS must proceed with SGA → make explicit decision (see Obstetric tab)</li></ul></div></div><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel bn">NOT VENTILATING</div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="box bdanger" onclick="tog('d8b')"><div class="t"><span class="badge">PLAN C</span>Optimise Face Mask Ventilation</div><div class="s">2-person technique · OPA/NPA · PEEP · Sugammadex?</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d8b"><h4>Plan C — Face Mask Ventilation</h4><ul><li>Two-person BVM: one clinician maintains two-hand EC-clamp mask seal,
second squeezes bag</li><li>Insert oropharyngeal airway (OPA) and/or nasopharyngeal airway (NPA)</li><li>PEEP valve 5–10 cmH₂O to maintain oxygenation</li><li><b>Sugammadex 16 mg/kg IV</b>if rocuronium used — reverses neuromuscular blockade in~3 minutes,
allowing wakeup attempt (not effective for succinylcholine)</li><li>If even partial ventilation achievable → buys time for senior help and planning</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bcico" onclick="tog('d9')"><div class="t">🚨 CICO — Cannot Intubate,
Cannot Oxygenate</div><div class="s">PLAN D → Immediate Front-of-Neck Access (FONA) — Do not delay</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="d9"><h4>PLAN D — Emergency Front-of-Neck Access (DAS 2015)</h4><p style="margin-bottom:8px;font-weight:600;color:#c0392b">Do not delay FONA. Each minute of hypoxia causes irreversible neurological damage. (NAP4, Cook et al., 2011)</p><ul><li><b>Scalpel–Bougie–Tube technique (DAS 2015 recommended):</b><ol style="margin-top:5px"><li>Extend neck;
identify cricothyroid membrane (CTM) using "laryngeal handshake" </li><li>Horizontal stab incision through skin <b>and</b>CTM with size 10 scalpel</li><li>Hook caudal tracheal ring with finger or tracheal hook;
maintain caudal traction</li><li>Insert bougie caudally through CTM incision into trachea</li><li>Railroad 6.0 mm cuffed ETT over bougie into trachea</li><li>Inflate cuff,
ventilate,
confirm with waveform capnography</li><li>Secure tube;
obtain CXR</li></ol></li><li><b>Pediatric CICO:</b>needle cricothyrotomy preferred &lt;
8 yrs (CTM too small for scalpel technique) — see Pediatric tab</li><li><b>Obstetric CICO:</b>same scalpel-bougie-tube;
CTM may be harder to palpate — use ultrasound if immediately available</li></ul><p style="margin-top:8px"><span class="tag d">Declare CICO aloud</span><span class="tag d">Call ENT/Surgery</span><span class="tag d">Document fully</span><span class="tag d">Debrief team</span></p></div></div></div><!-- /flow --></div><!-- /tab-algo --><!-- ═══════════════════════════ ASSESSMENT TOOLS ═══════════════════════════ --><div id="tab-assess" class="tab-content"><div class="sh">MACOCHA Score — Difficult Intubation in ICU</div><p style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;text-align:center;margin-bottom:10px">De Jong et al.,
