identify CTM by <b>"laryngeal handshake" </b>(thumb and middle finger stabilise thyroid cartilage; index finger identifies CTM)</li><li>Horizontal stab incision through skin <b>AND</b>CTM with size 10 scalpel (single confident motion)</li><li>Hook caudal tracheal ring with hooked index finger or tracheal hook;
maintain firm <b>caudal</b>traction</li><li>Insert bougie caudally through the CTM incision into trachea (feel for tracheal rings)</li><li>Railroad <b>6.0 mm cuffed ETT</b>over bougie into trachea</li><li>Remove bougie;
inflate cuff;
ventilate immediately</li><li>Confirm with <b>waveform capnography</b>;
secure tube;
obtain CXR</li></ol></div><table class="tbl"><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Evidence Level</th><th>Time to Ventilation</th><th>Key Limitation</th></tr><tr><td><b>Scalpel-bougie-tube</b></td><td>DAS 2015 Grade B;
highest success in cadaveric studies</td><td>~30–60 s (trained)</td><td>Requires deliberate practice on manikins/cadavers</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial kits (Melker, Portex)</td><td>Acceptable Seldinger-technique alternative</td><td>~60–90 s</td><td>More procedural steps under hypoxic stress</td></tr><tr><td>Needle cric (14G IV cannula)</td><td>NAP4: 65% failure rate in adults;
temporising only</td><td>~30 s (if successful)</td><td>CO₂ retention,
