20G in neonates;
frequent barotrauma</td></tr></table><div class="alert y" style="margin-top:14px"><b>NAP4 Key Findings</b>(Cook et al., Br J Anaesth 2011 — 184 major airway complications): <ul style="padding-left:16px;margin-top:6px"><li>25% of CICO events resulted in death or hypoxic brain damage</li><li>Majority of deaths associated with <b>delayed decision to perform FONA</b></li><li>Needle cricothyrotomy failed in majority of adult cases when used as primary CICO rescue</li><li>Poor identification of CTM was a recurrent failure point — practice anatomy identification regularly</li></ul></div></div><!-- ═══════════════════════════ PEDIATRIC ═══════════════════════════ --><div id="tab-peds" class="tab-content"><div class="sh p">🧒 Pediatric Airway — Adaptations &amp;
Algorithm</div><p style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;text-align:center;margin-bottom:12px">Black et al.,
<i>Paediatr Anaesth</i>2015 (DAS Paediatric) · AHA PALS 2020 · Weiss et al.,
<i>Lancet</i>2009 · Riva et al.,
<i>Paediatr Anaesth</i>2018 </p><div class="alert p"><b>Key Anatomical Differences Versus Adults — Clinical Impact</b><ul style="margin-top:8px;padding-left:17px;line-height:1.9"><li><b>Large occiput</b>→ in neonates/infants,
places neck in flexion when supine;
requires <b>shoulder roll</b>to achieve neutral/sniffing position. Children &gt;
2 yrs → standard pillow under head</li><li><b>Proportionally larger tongue</b>relative to oral cavity → obstructs airway more easily;
OPA/NPA critical;
jaw thrust highly effective</li><li><b>Anterior,
cephalad larynx</b>(C3–C4 at birth vs. C5–C6 in adults) → steeper angle for laryngoscopy;
blade must be directed more anteriorly</li><li><b>Omega-shaped,
floppy epiglottis</b>in infants → straight (Miller) blade preferred &lt;
2 yrs to directly elevate epiglottis and visualise glottis</li><li><b>Narrowest point at cricoid ring</b>(subglottic) in children &lt;
10 yrs — not at glottis as in adults → cuffed tube sizing and cuff inflation pressure critical (≤20 cmH₂O)</li><li><b>Short trachea</b>(~5–6 cm in neonates) → right main-stem intubation common;
always confirm bilateral breath sounds immediately after intubation</li><li><b>Higher O₂ consumption per kg+smaller FRC</b>→ desaturation occurs far faster than adults,
especially in neonates and infants</li><li><b>Obligate nasal breathers</b>(infants &lt; 4–6 months) → nasal obstruction from blood/secretions is dangerous;
NPA+gentle suction critical</li></ul></div><div class="sh p">Equipment Sizing by Age / Weight</div><p style="font-size:0.71rem;color:#555;text-align:center;margin-bottom:9px">Cuffed ETT formula: (Age/4)+3.5 · Depth at lip (cm): (Age/2)+12 · Weight formula: (Age+4) × 2 kg (Luscombe &amp; Owens) · Always have Broselow tape available</p><table class="tbl p"><tr><th>Age / Weight</th><th>ETT Size (cuffed)</th><th>ETT Depth (lip)</th><th>i-gel / LMA Size</th><th>Laryngoscope Blade</th></tr><tr><td>Premature (&lt; 1 kg)</td><td>2.5 (uncuffed)</td><td>5.5–6.5 cm</td><td>i-gel 1 (≥2 kg only)</td><td>Miller 00</td></tr><tr><td>Neonate 1–2 kg</td><td>3.0 (uncuffed or cuffed)</td><td>7–8 cm</td><td>i-gel 1</td><td>Miller 0</td></tr><tr><td>Neonate 2–3 kg</td><td>3.0–3.5 (cuffed)</td><td>8–9 cm</td><td>i-gel 1</td><td>Miller 0</td></tr><tr><td>3–12 months</td><td>3.5 (cuffed)</td><td>10–11 cm</td><td>i-gel 1.5</td><td>Miller 1</td></tr><tr><td>1–2 years</td><td>4.0 (cuffed)</td><td>11–12 cm</td><td>i-gel 2</td><td>Miller 1 / Mac 1</td></tr><tr><td>2–5 years</td><td>(Age/4)+3.5 cuffed</td><td>(Age/2)+12 cm</td><td>i-gel 2 / 2.5</td><td>Miller 2 / Mac 2</td></tr><tr><td>6–10 years</td><td>(Age/4)+3.5 cuffed</td><td>(Age/2)+12 cm</td><td>i-gel 2.5 / 3</td><td>Mac 2</td></tr><tr><td>&gt;
