useful in status epilepticus;
cardiovascular depression in volume-depleted states;
limited global availability</td></tr><tr><td><b>Succinylcholine</b></td><td><b>2 mg/kg IV</b>(≤10 kg) · 1.5 mg/kg IV (&gt; 10 kg) · 4 mg/kg IM (if no IV)</td><td>⚠ <b>Higher mg/kg dose required</b>in young children (larger volume of distribution). <b>Pre-treat with atropine 0.02 mg/kg IV</b>(min 0.1 mg, max 0.5 mg) in all &lt;
5 yrs and in older children receiving repeat doses — prevents vagally-mediated bradycardia. CI: same as adults+undiagnosed muscle disease (risk of hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rocuronium</b></td><td>1.2 mg/kg IV (RSI dose)</td><td>Preferred NMB when succinylcholine contraindicated;
fully reversible with sugammadex;
onset slightly slower than succinylcholine but reliable at 1.2 mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td><b>Atropine</b></td><td>0.02 mg/kg IV (min 0.1 mg, max 0.5 mg)</td><td>Pre-RSI in all &lt;
5 yrs;
before laryngoscopy in neonates (high vagal tone → bradycardia common);
before succinylcholine in older children</td></tr><tr class="hrow"><td><b>Sugammadex</b></td><td>16 mg/kg IV (rescue CICO) · 4 mg/kg IV (routine reversal TOF ≥2)</td><td>Evidence supports use in children ≥2 yrs;
