1 yr</b></td><td>Needle cricothyrotomy (20–22G IV cannula)</td><td>20G cannula+jet ventilator 5 psi</td><td>CTM not palpable and too small for any surgical technique</td></tr><tr><td><b>1–8 years</b></td><td>Needle cricothyrotomy (14–18G IV cannula)</td><td>14–18G cannula+jet ventilation</td><td>CTM identifiable but narrow;
scalpel not safe at this age</td></tr><tr><td><b>&gt;
8 years</b></td><td>Scalpel-bougie-tube (adult DAS technique)</td><td>5.0–6.0 mm cuffed ETT via CTM</td><td>CTM adult-sized;
standard technique applies</td></tr><tr class="hrow"><td colspan="4"><b>Jet ventilation:</b>5–10 psi in young children;
I:E ratio 1:4;
watch for barotrauma,
hyperinflation,
and inadequate CO₂ clearance. Limit to &lt;
30 min — this is a bridge to definitive airway only.</td></tr></table><div class="sh p">Pediatric Difficult Airway Prediction</div><div class="alert p"><p>Unlike adults,
no validated scoring system exists specifically for pediatric difficult airway. Key red flags include:</p><ul style="padding-left:17px;margin-top:7px;line-height:1.9"><li><b>Syndromes:</b>Pierre Robin,
Treacher Collins,
Down syndrome (macroglossia + atlantoaxial instability),
Goldenhar,
Mucopolysaccharidoses</li><li><b>Acquired:</b>epiglottitis,
croup (subglottic oedema),
burns,
Ludwig's angina, foreign body</li>
 <li><b>Anatomical:</b>micrognathia,
macroglossia,
cleft palate,
choanal atresia,
laryngomalacia</li><li>History of previous difficult intubation or prolonged laryngoscopy in child's medical notes</li>
 <li>Always involve senior paediatric anaesthetist early;
consider ENT standby for known syndromic children</li></ul></div></div><!-- ═══════════════════════════ OBSTETRIC ═══════════════════════════ --><div id="tab-obs" class="tab-content"><div class="sh o">🤰 Obstetric Difficult Airway — OAA/DAS 2015</div><p style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;text-align:center;margin-bottom:12px">Mushambi et al.,
<i>Anaesthesia</i>2015 · Kinsella et al.,
<i>Anaesthesia</i>2015 · MBRRACE-UK 2022 (Knight et al., <i>BJOG</i> 2022) · Hawkins et al.,
<i>Anesthesiology</i>1997 </p><div class="alert obs"><b>Why Obstetric Airway Is Uniquely Hazardous</b><ul style="margin-top:8px;padding-left:17px;line-height:1.9"><li><b>Failed intubation rate 1:224</b>obstetric GA (vs. 1:2, 500 general surgical — Kinsella et al., Anaesthesia 2015)</li><li><b>Rapid O₂ desaturation:</b>FRC reduced by 20%+O₂ consumption increased 20% at term → critical hypoxia in &lt;
3 minutes without preoxygenation</li><li><b>Full stomach / aspiration risk:</b>progesterone reduces lower oesophageal sphincter (LOS) tone;
gastric motility delayed in labour;
Mendelson syndrome (acid aspiration pneumonitis) risk</li><li><b>Mallampati worsens 1–2 classes during labour</b>due to mucosal oedema from fluid loading,
Valsalva,
and pushing — Kuczkowski et al.,
J Clin Anesth 2003</li><li><b>Airway mucosal oedema</b>from IV fluid loading,
pre-eclampsia (especially),
protracted pushing</li><li><b>Breast enlargement</b>in late pregnancy may physically obstruct insertion of standard laryngoscope handle — use short-handled blade or polio blade</li><li><b>Aortocaval compression</b>in supine position — reduces venous return and cardiac output → must maintain left lateral tilt 15° at all times</li><li><b>MBRRACE-UK 2022:</b>airway-related deaths remain a leading cause of direct anaesthetic maternal mortality in the UK</li><li><b>GA mortality 17× higher than regional anaesthesia</b>for caesarean section — Hawkins et al.,
Anesthesiology 1997</li></ul></div><div class="sh o">Physiological Changes Affecting Airway Management</div><div class="cgrid"><div class="cgrid-item"><h5 style="color:#8e44ad">🫁 Respiratory Changes</h5><ul style="padding-left:14px;line-height:1.9;font-size:0.72rem"><li>FRC ↓ 20% (diaphragmatic elevation by gravid uterus)</li><li>O₂ consumption ↑ 20% (metabolic demand)</li><li>Tidal volume ↑ 40% (progesterone-driven)</li><li>Respiratory rate ↑ 15%</li><li>PaCO₂ ↓~32 mmHg (compensated respiratory alkalosis)</li><li>PaO₂ ↑ slightly due to hyperventilation</li></ul></div><div class="cgrid-item"><h5 style="color:#8e44ad">👄 Airway Anatomy</h5><ul style="padding-left:14px;line-height:1.9;font-size:0.72rem"><li>Mallampati class increases 1–2 grades</li><li>Mucosal oedema → smaller glottis aperture</li><li>Use smaller ETT: <b>6.0–7.0 mm cuffed</b></li><li>Short-handled or polio-blade laryngoscope</li><li>Pre-eclampsia → severe glottic/supraglottic oedema possible</li><li>Nasal passages engorged → avoid nasal intubation</li></ul></div><div class="cgrid-item"><h5 style="color:#8e44ad">🍽 GI / Aspiration Risk</h5><ul style="padding-left:14px;line-height:1.9;font-size:0.72rem"><li>Gastroparesis in active labour</li><li>LOS tone reduced (progesterone)</li><li>Intragastric pressure increased</li><li><b>Na citrate 30 mL PO</b>immediately pre-GA</li><li>Ranitidine 150 mg PO / omeprazole 40 mg PO pre-elective GA</li><li>Metoclopramide 10 mg IV if nausea</li></ul></div><div class="cgrid-item"><h5 style="color:#8e44ad">🫀 Cardiovascular</h5><ul style="padding-left:14px;line-height:1.9;font-size:0.72rem"><li>Aortocaval compression in supine position</li><li><b>Left lateral tilt 15° mandatory</b>(wedge under right hip)</li><li>CO increases ↑ 40% at term</li><li>Blood volume ↑ 40–45% (↑ plasma &gt; ↑ RBCs → dilutional anaemia)</li><li>HR ↑ 15–20 bpm at term</li></ul></div></div><div class="sh o">OAA/DAS 2015 — Obstetric Airway Algorithm</div><div class="flow" style="margin-top:10px"><div class="step-w"><div class="box bobs" onclick="tog('ob1')"><div class="t"><span class="badge o">OB 1</span>Pre-Anaesthetic Assessment — ALL Obstetric Patients</div><div class="s">At booking AND reassess in active labour</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob1"><h4>Obstetric Airway Assessment (OAA/DAS 2015)</h4><ul><li>Assess at <b>booking</b>(first trimester) and again on arrival to labour ward (Mallampati worsens significantly)</li><li>Document: Mallampati class,
mouth opening,
neck mobility,
BMI,
dentition,
previous difficult intubation,
snoring/OSA</li><li><b>Pre-eclampsia:</b>assess frequently — airway can worsen rapidly;
early regional anaesthesia strongly preferred</li><li><b>BMI &gt;
40:</b>highest risk category for failed intubation+aspiration;
establish regional early;
notify senior anaesthetist</li><li>Ensure resuscitation-ready environment for all obstetric GA (senior anaesthetist, full airway trolley, VL, HFNO available)</li><li>Consider early epidural insertion in all high-risk patients to avoid emergency GA</li></ul><p style="margin-top:6px"><span class="tag obs">Na Citrate 30 mL PO if time allows</span><span class="tag obs">Ranitidine / Omeprazole</span><span class="tag obs">Metoclopramide if nausea</span></p></div></div><div class="arr p">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bobs" onclick="tog('ob2')"><div class="t"><span class="badge o">OB 2</span>Regional Anaesthesia — Strongly Preferred</div><div class="s">Spinal · Epidural · CSE · Avoid GA wherever possible</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob2"><h4>Regional Anaesthesia Evidence</h4><ul><li><b>Spinal anaesthesia:</b>fastest for category 1 CS when regional is possible;
onset 3–5 min with hyperbaric bupivacaine 0.5% 2.2–2.5 mL+fentanyl 25 mcg+morphine 100 mcg</li><li><b>Epidural top-up:</b>if labour epidural already in situ;
15–20 mL lidocaine 2%+adrenaline ± fentanyl 50–100 mcg;
onset 10–15 min;
faster than establishing new spinal in urgent cases</li><li><b>CSE (combined spinal-epidural):</b>benefits of both techniques;
useful when duration uncertain or high-risk cases requiring flexibility</li><li><b>Evidence:</b>GA mortality risk 17× higher than regional for CS (Hawkins et al., Anesthesiology 1997)</li><li>Early epidural insertion in high-risk patients (predicted difficult airway, pre-eclampsia, obesity, multiple pregnancy) to avoid emergency GA</li></ul><p style="margin-top:6px"><span class="tag obs">Absolute CI to regional: coagulopathy (platelets &lt; 80),
patient refusal,
active infection at site,
haemodynamic instability</span></p></div></div><div class="arr p">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bwarn" onclick="tog('ob3')" style="border-radius:40px"><div class="t">⚠ GA Required?</div><div class="s">Category 1 emergency · Regional failed/contraindicated · Maternal collapse</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob3"><h4>Indications for Obstetric GA</h4><ul><li>Category 1 CS with immediate threat to maternal or fetal life and insufficient time for regional</li><li>Failed regional block despite top-up attempts (conversion rate ~1.5–3%)</li><li>Contraindications to regional: coagulopathy (DIC, HELLP, thrombocytopaenia &lt; 80),
patient refusal,
active sepsis at site</li><li>Maternal cardiac arrest → perimortem CS under GA</li><li>Massive haemorrhage with haemodynamic instability precluding regional</li></ul></div></div><div class="branch"><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel by">If time allows</div><div class="arr p">↓</div><div class="box bobs" onclick="tog('ob4a')"><div class="t">Anticipated Difficult — Awake Technique</div><div class="s">Awake FOB · Awake VL · Awake tracheostomy</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob4a"><h4>Awake Intubation in Obstetrics</h4><ul><li>Use when <b>predicted difficult airway AND time permits</b>(not true category 1 emergency)</li><li><b>Awake FOB preferred</b>— maintains spontaneous breathing and airway tone throughout;
fetal O₂ delivery maintained</li><li>Topicalise carefully: 4% lidocaine spray — lower ceiling in pregnancy (max ~4–5 mg/kg; reduced protein binding changes free drug levels)</li><li>Sedation: <b>Remifentanil TCI</b>(target 1–2 ng/mL, titrate carefully) — fetal respiratory depression if &gt;
2 ng/mL maintained;
have naloxone for neonate</li><li><b>Dexmedetomidine</b>emerging evidence;
crosses placenta less than benzodiazepines;
minimal fetal respiratory effects</li><li>Avoid benzodiazepines — significant neonatal respiratory depression</li><li>Document clearly;
communicate to obstetric and neonatal teams;
ensure neonatologist aware of sedation given</li></ul></div></div><div class="bcol"><div class="blabel bn">Emergency GA</div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="box bobs2" onclick="tog('ob4b')"><div class="t"><span class="badge o">OB RSI</span>Modified RSI for Obstetrics</div><div class="s">Left tilt · Cricoid pressure · Preoxygenate · VL first-line</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob4b"><h4>Obstetric RSI Protocol (OAA/DAS 2015)</h4><ul><li><b>Left lateral tilt 15°</b>(firm wedge under right hip) throughout — prevents aortocaval compression</li><li><b>Preoxygenation:</b>3 min tidal breathing on tight-fitting mask at 10–15 L/min OR 4 × vital capacity breaths;
target SpO₂ 100% and EtO₂ ≥0.87 (87%);
HFNO 60 L/min maintained during laryngoscopy if available</li><li><b>Cricoid pressure (Sellick manoeuvre):</b>10 N awake,
increased to 30 N on induction — OAA/DAS 2015 maintains recommendation despite controversy;
<b>release immediately</b>if it worsens laryngoscopy view or prevents effective BVM ventilation</li><li><b>Induction agents:</b><ul><li>Thiopentone 4–5 mg/kg (historical gold standard; reduces ICP; haemodynamic stability)</li><li>Propofol 2 mg/kg (acceptable; slight cardiovascular depression)</li><li>Ketamine 1–1.5 mg/kg (hypotension, asthma, haemorrhage)</li></ul></li><li><b>NMB:</b>Succinylcholine 1.5 mg/kg preferred (fastest offset ~10 min — reduces fetal exposure; allows return of spontaneous ventilation if CICO) OR rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg (with sugammadex 16 mg/kg available for rescue)</li><li><b>ETT size 6.0–7.0 mm cuffed</b>(smaller glottis from oedema);
have 5.5 mm immediately available</li><li><b>Video laryngoscopy as first-line</b>— OAA/DAS 2015 recommendation;
reduces failed intubation rate</li><li>Short-handled or polio-blade laryngoscope to navigate around enlarged breasts</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bwarn" onclick="tog('ob5')" style="border-radius:40px"><div class="t">⚠ Failed Obstetric Intubation?</div><div class="s">Incidence: 1 in 224 obstetric GA cases (Kinsella et al., 2015)</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob5"><h4>Context and Response</h4><ul><li>Failed intubation: 1:224 obstetric GA vs. 1:2,
500 general surgical — 10× higher risk</li><li>Higher rates in: BMI &gt;
40,
pre-eclampsia,
emergency category 1,
failed regional conversion under time pressure</li><li><b>Declare "failed intubation" aloud after maximum 2 attempts</b></li><li>Do not persist — escalating airway trauma worsens all subsequent options</li><li>Maintain oxygenation as the immediate priority</li></ul></div></div><div class="arr p">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bobs" onclick="tog('ob6')"><div class="t"><span class="badge o">OB CRITICAL</span>Wake Up vs. Proceed with Surgery?</div><div class="s">Weigh maternal AND fetal risk — most critical obstetric airway decision</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob6"><h4>The OAA/DAS 2015 "Wake vs. Proceed" Decision Framework</h4><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.72rem;margin-top:6px"><tr style="background:#8e44ad;color:#fff"><th style="padding:6px 9px;text-align:left">Factor</th><th style="padding:6px 9px;text-align:left">Favour WAKE UP (abort GA, plan definitive airway)</th><th style="padding:6px 9px;text-align:left">Favour PROCEED (maintain anaesthesia, use SGA)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee"><b>Fetal condition</b></td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">Reassuring CTG;
no immediate fetal compromise</td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">Severe fetal distress;
cord prolapse;
massive abruption;
fetal bradycardia</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee"><b>Maternal condition</b></td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">Haemodynamically stable;
oxygenating well via SGA or mask</td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">Maternal haemorrhage;
cannot wake safely;
CICO situation</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee"><b>Airway status</b></td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">SGA ventilating effectively;
oxygenating;
no CICO</td><td style="padding:5px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee">Cannot ventilate via SGA or mask;
CICO imminent</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px 9px"><b>Surgical stage</b></td><td style="padding:5px 9px">Not yet incised;
early in procedure</td><td style="padding:5px 9px">Uterus already open;
baby partially delivered;
surgeon cannot safely stop</td></tr></table><p style="margin-top:9px"><span class="tag obs">OAA/DAS 2015: if oxygenating via SGA and no immediate fetal threat → waking up is usually the safer maternal option</span></p><p style="margin-top:5px;font-size:0.71rem;color:#666">If proceeding with SGA: use 2nd-gen SGA;
gastric drainage;
light plane of anaesthesia only;
senior anaesthetist must be present;
plan definitive airway at end of surgery</p></div></div><div class="arr r">↓</div><div class="step-w"><div class="box bobs2" onclick="tog('ob7')"><div class="t">🚨 Obstetric CICO → FONA</div><div class="s">Scalpel-bougie-tube · Maternal life is priority · Declare,
call for help</div><div class="hint">Click to expand ▾</div></div><div class="dp" id="ob7"><h4>Obstetric CICO — Front-of-Neck Access (OAA/DAS 2015)</h4><ul><li><b>Maternal life takes absolute priority</b>in CICO — delivery of fetus is secondary to establishing maternal oxygenation</li><li>Scalpel-bougie-tube as per DAS 2015;
use 6.0 mm cuffed ETT</li><li><b>CTM identification may be harder:</b>neck oedema,
obesity,
engorgement of structures → use <b>ultrasound if immediately available</b>(pre-procedure marking strongly recommended in high-risk patients)</li><li><b>Release cricoid pressure</b>before attempting FONA — it distorts anatomy</li><li><b>Perimortem caesarean section (PMCS):</b>if maternal cardiac arrest occurs — deliver within 4–5 minutes of arrest to improve maternal resuscitation outcomes and prevent fetal anoxia (Katz et al., NEJM 2005)</li><li>Post-event: immediate team debrief;
full documentation;
notify neonatal team of events;
arrange Difficult Airway Alert card and follow-up for mother;
report through critical incident system</li></ul><p style="margin-top:7px"><span class="tag d">Declare CICO aloud</span><span class="tag d">Call senior anaesthetist</span><span class="tag d">Call ENT/Surgery</span><span class="tag d">Debrief entire team</span></p></div></div></div><!-- /obs flow --><div class="sh o" style="margin-top:22px">Pre-Eclampsia — Specific Airway Considerations</div><div class="alert y"><b>Pre-eclampsia creates the highest-risk obstetric airway scenario</b><ul style="padding-left:17px;margin-top:8px;line-height:1.9"><li><b>Severe glottic and supraglottic oedema</b>— Mallampati can reach class IV even in patients with no prior airway difficulty;
